WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.033 --> 00:00:01.901
How on earth in 20, 22

00:00:01.901 --> 00:00:03.570
did they find a panel of jurors

00:00:03.570 --> 00:00:05.405
who are truly impartial

00:00:05.405 --> 00:00:07.374
when it comes to Donald Trump?

00:00:07.374 --> 00:00:08.575
That's what I was wondering

00:00:08.575 --> 00:00:09.242
during this week's

00:00:09.242 --> 00:00:10.143
jury selection

00:00:10.143 --> 00:00:10.810
for the Trump

00:00:10.810 --> 00:00:12.078
Organization tax fraud

00:00:12.078 --> 00:00:14.214
trial in Manhattan Supreme Court,

00:00:14.214 --> 00:00:15.782
which took three days.

00:00:15.782 --> 00:00:17.684
12 jurors were chosen

00:00:17.684 --> 00:00:18.585
two of the Trump

00:00:18.585 --> 00:00:19.919
Payroll Corporation Defense

00:00:19.919 --> 00:00:21.321
Lawyers will join me in just a moment

00:00:21.321 --> 00:00:23.023
to answer that question.

00:00:23.023 --> 00:00:23.790
Trump himself

00:00:23.790 --> 00:00:25.291
isn't personally charged

00:00:25.291 --> 00:00:26.493
in this case against

00:00:26.493 --> 00:00:27.060
his company,

00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:28.061
isn't expected

00:00:28.061 --> 00:00:29.763
to show up at the courthouse

00:00:29.763 --> 00:00:30.530
unlike his former

00:00:30.530 --> 00:00:31.931
chief financial officer, Allen

00:00:31.931 --> 00:00:33.333
Weisselberg, who pled guilty

00:00:33.333 --> 00:00:34.367
and is expected to be

00:00:34.367 --> 00:00:35.735
the state star witness.

00:00:35.735 --> 00:00:38.371
But just as he looms over the midterms,

00:00:38.972 --> 00:00:40.373
despite not being on the ballot,

00:00:40.373 --> 00:00:41.107
he can't help

00:00:41.107 --> 00:00:43.443
but be a specter in the courtroom.

00:00:43.443 --> 00:00:44.944
Question 29,

00:00:44.944 --> 00:00:46.613
the juror questionnaire form

00:00:46.613 --> 00:00:48.114
got to the crux of it.

00:00:48.114 --> 00:00:50.483
Do you have any strong opinions or firmly

00:00:50.483 --> 00:00:51.818
held beliefs about former

00:00:51.818 --> 00:00:52.919
President Donald J.

00:00:52.919 --> 00:00:55.021
Trump, either positive or negative

00:00:55.021 --> 00:00:56.956
that would interfere with your ability

00:00:56.956 --> 00:00:58.992
to be a fair and impartial juror?

00:00:59.559 --> 00:01:00.827
As lead prosecutor

00:01:00.827 --> 00:01:03.096
Susan Hoffman's, you're told the court.

00:01:03.096 --> 00:01:04.798
If we were to strike every juror

00:01:04.798 --> 00:01:05.765
who had a negative opinion

00:01:05.765 --> 00:01:06.533
about Donald Trump,

00:01:06.533 --> 00:01:08.134
we wouldn't be able to get a jury at all.

00:01:09.235 --> 00:01:11.304
And you have to believe other prosecutors

00:01:11.805 --> 00:01:13.706
who may be considering prosecuting

00:01:13.706 --> 00:01:14.941
Trump himself

00:01:14.941 --> 00:01:16.643
are closely watching this trial,

00:01:16.643 --> 00:01:18.078
this process.

00:01:18.078 --> 00:01:18.778
When Judge

00:01:18.778 --> 00:01:20.447
Juan Mershon first informed

00:01:20.447 --> 00:01:22.115
130 potential jurors

00:01:22.115 --> 00:01:23.683
about details of this case,

00:01:23.683 --> 00:01:25.718
many waved their hands in the air

00:01:25.718 --> 00:01:27.720
to request private screening by the judge

00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:28.621
and lawyers

00:01:28.621 --> 00:01:30.723
about reasons they could not serve.

00:01:30.723 --> 00:01:33.293
More than half were then dismissed.

00:01:33.293 --> 00:01:34.894
One man was excused after saying

00:01:34.894 --> 00:01:37.397
Trump made him, quote, sick to his guts.

00:01:37.397 --> 00:01:38.298
Another was gone

00:01:38.298 --> 00:01:40.433
after calling Trump a demonstrative liar.

00:01:40.467 --> 00:01:42.502
One woman said, He's guilty.

00:01:42.502 --> 00:01:43.236
In my mind,

00:01:43.236 --> 00:01:44.404
whatever the case is,

00:01:44.404 --> 00:01:45.872
anything he does, anything

00:01:45.872 --> 00:01:47.440
his corporation does.

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:50.210
Of the 12 selected on Thursday.

00:01:50.577 --> 00:01:53.012
Three or a quarter

00:01:53.012 --> 00:01:53.746
openly said

00:01:53.746 --> 00:01:54.681
they are not fans

00:01:54.681 --> 00:01:55.615
of the former president,

00:01:55.615 --> 00:01:56.549
but nevertheless said

00:01:56.549 --> 00:01:58.284
they could be fair and impartial.

00:01:58.284 --> 00:01:59.719
As jurors.

00:01:59.719 --> 00:02:01.721
The man who would become juror number

00:02:01.721 --> 00:02:02.222
eight said,

00:02:02.222 --> 00:02:04.157
Honestly, I used to think he was funny

00:02:04.157 --> 00:02:05.492
before he was president.

00:02:05.492 --> 00:02:07.193
Then he started acting a little crazy

00:02:07.193 --> 00:02:08.928
and narcissistic

00:02:08.928 --> 00:02:09.996
That's the only reason

00:02:09.996 --> 00:02:11.464
I didn't like him as president.

00:02:11.464 --> 00:02:13.266
Not so much policy.

00:02:13.266 --> 00:02:14.701
Joining me now are two veteran

00:02:14.701 --> 00:02:15.602
Philadelphia lawyers

00:02:15.602 --> 00:02:16.336
working for the Trump

00:02:16.336 --> 00:02:18.037
Payroll Corporation defense team.

00:02:18.037 --> 00:02:20.440
William Brennan and Michael Van Der Veen,

00:02:20.874 --> 00:02:22.675
both of whom also represented Trump

00:02:22.675 --> 00:02:24.210
during his second impeachment.

00:02:24.210 --> 00:02:26.212
With Van Der Veen taking the lead role.

00:02:26.246 --> 00:02:27.147
You'll remember him

00:02:30.116 --> 00:02:31.317
putting in your evidence.

00:02:31.317 --> 00:02:32.452
So I started to be able

00:02:32.452 --> 00:02:33.686
to get looking at it.

00:02:33.686 --> 00:02:35.955
You need to stop.

00:02:36.389 --> 00:02:38.024
There was nothing fun here, Mr.

00:02:38.024 --> 00:02:39.859
Raskin. We aren't having fun here.

00:02:39.859 --> 00:02:40.860
This is about the most

00:02:40.860 --> 00:02:41.194
you're being

00:02:41.194 --> 00:02:42.562
you're starting this trial

00:02:42.562 --> 00:02:44.030
with a quarter of the jurors

00:02:44.030 --> 00:02:45.732
saying they don't like. And I get it.

00:02:45.732 --> 00:02:47.634
You represent a corporate entity,

00:02:47.634 --> 00:02:48.501
but come on,

00:02:48.501 --> 00:02:49.802
they don't like Donald Trump.

00:02:49.802 --> 00:02:51.571
How can you win that?

00:02:53.039 --> 00:02:55.108
Well, the first

00:02:56.042 --> 00:02:56.743
you'll win it

00:02:56.743 --> 00:02:58.511
by putting the facts into the case

00:02:58.511 --> 00:03:00.446
and putting your defenses on.

00:03:00.446 --> 00:03:01.247
But what we did

00:03:01.247 --> 00:03:03.883
in the jury selection is we tried to

00:03:03.883 --> 00:03:06.186
separate Donald Trump from the company.

00:03:06.819 --> 00:03:09.189
If he's not a defendant in this case.

00:03:10.423 --> 00:03:12.258
They've investigated his companies

00:03:12.258 --> 00:03:13.326
thoroughly.

00:03:13.359 --> 00:03:14.194
They didn't

00:03:14.194 --> 00:03:15.962
charge him with any criminal conduct.

00:03:15.962 --> 00:03:17.864
And and the running of the company

00:03:17.864 --> 00:03:19.365
and the folks that were doing

00:03:19.365 --> 00:03:21.501
the acting for the company

00:03:21.501 --> 00:03:24.103
are the focus of of the trial

00:03:24.571 --> 00:03:26.306
and I think will be

00:03:26.306 --> 00:03:27.574
what carries the day for us.

00:03:28.841 --> 00:03:29.809
Bill Brennan, you've been

00:03:29.809 --> 00:03:30.643
quoted in the media

00:03:30.643 --> 00:03:33.046
as saying this is a garden variety tax

00:03:33.046 --> 00:03:33.980
fraud case,

00:03:33.980 --> 00:03:34.914
but still,

00:03:34.914 --> 00:03:37.183
won't it become a referendum on Trump?

00:03:38.918 --> 00:03:40.920
Michael, that's out of my control.

00:03:40.920 --> 00:03:43.022
I've been doing this for 35 years.

00:03:43.022 --> 00:03:45.992
This is a garden variety tax

00:03:45.992 --> 00:03:47.460
fraud criminal case,

00:03:47.460 --> 00:03:49.128
and that's how I'm going to try it.

00:03:49.128 --> 00:03:51.130
And we spent a long time

00:03:51.130 --> 00:03:53.866
this week trying to select

00:03:53.866 --> 00:03:56.636
12 jurors who gave their word

00:03:56.970 --> 00:03:58.338
that they'll give us a fair shot.

00:03:58.338 --> 00:04:00.540
And I believe in the jury system

00:04:00.840 --> 00:04:02.175
and I believe that

00:04:02.175 --> 00:04:03.876
when people get into that jury box

00:04:03.876 --> 00:04:05.211
in that jury room,

00:04:05.211 --> 00:04:07.480
they're their best instincts take over.

00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:09.382
And I take them at their word

00:04:09.382 --> 00:04:10.550
that they'll give us a fair shake.

00:04:10.550 --> 00:04:12.018
You know, we're presumed innocent.

00:04:12.018 --> 00:04:15.221
And I think that the you know, we'll

00:04:15.822 --> 00:04:17.390
we'll have a fair jury.

00:04:18.858 --> 00:04:19.726
Mike Michael Van Der

00:04:19.726 --> 00:04:21.861
Veen so if I'm a prospective juror

00:04:21.961 --> 00:04:22.595
and I'm asked,

00:04:22.595 --> 00:04:23.730
what do you think of Donald Trump?

00:04:23.730 --> 00:04:25.231
And I disparage him, in fact,

00:04:25.231 --> 00:04:27.934
maybe I even used the word despise.

00:04:28.668 --> 00:04:29.602
How does it work?

00:04:29.602 --> 00:04:32.005
Am I then asked a question by the court?

00:04:32.005 --> 00:04:33.106
Well, you know, Mr.

00:04:33.106 --> 00:04:34.774
Smerconish, could you nevertheless

00:04:34.774 --> 00:04:36.843
put aside those feelings?

00:04:36.843 --> 00:04:37.977
And if I say, yeah,

00:04:37.977 --> 00:04:39.412
I could put aside all that, then

00:04:39.412 --> 00:04:41.014
then I'm in

00:04:42.615 --> 00:04:44.617
Yeah, that's basically it.

00:04:44.617 --> 00:04:46.419
And a little bit more expansive to Mike.

00:04:46.419 --> 00:04:47.020
What we

00:04:47.020 --> 00:04:47.620
what we do

00:04:47.620 --> 00:04:48.888
is we really start asking

00:04:48.888 --> 00:04:50.923
probing questions about

00:04:50.923 --> 00:04:51.824
the specifics

00:04:51.824 --> 00:04:54.394
of their feelings or emotions

00:04:55.128 --> 00:04:56.562
and talk to them

00:04:56.562 --> 00:04:58.097
about weighing the balance.

00:04:58.097 --> 00:04:59.966
But what's really most critical

00:04:59.966 --> 00:05:01.601
and what we rely on

00:05:01.601 --> 00:05:02.769
and what all lawyers

00:05:02.769 --> 00:05:03.703
rely on

00:05:03.703 --> 00:05:04.470
is once

00:05:04.470 --> 00:05:07.106
that jury is picked, seated and sworn in

00:05:07.874 --> 00:05:09.008
and they go back

00:05:09.008 --> 00:05:09.976
and they listen to the

00:05:09.976 --> 00:05:11.277
evidence and deliberate

00:05:12.545 --> 00:05:13.713
That door closes.

00:05:13.713 --> 00:05:15.882
And, boy, people try to do the best

00:05:15.882 --> 00:05:16.382
they can.

00:05:16.382 --> 00:05:18.484
They they put aside their prejudices.

00:05:18.484 --> 00:05:20.153
They put for the most part,

00:05:20.153 --> 00:05:22.155
put aside their biases.

00:05:22.155 --> 00:05:24.190
And try to be the best jurors they can.

00:05:24.190 --> 00:05:25.191
They follow.

00:05:25.191 --> 00:05:25.758
We find that

00:05:25.758 --> 00:05:27.193
they follow the judge's

00:05:27.193 --> 00:05:29.162
instructions closely

00:05:29.162 --> 00:05:31.230
and they try to be the best part

00:05:31.531 --> 00:05:34.300
of our system that they can be.

00:05:34.300 --> 00:05:35.501
Counselor Brennan.

00:05:35.501 --> 00:05:36.269
In the end,

00:05:36.269 --> 00:05:38.271
it's a self-certification, right?

00:05:38.271 --> 00:05:39.272
You have to

00:05:39.272 --> 00:05:40.907
the court has to take the word

00:05:40.907 --> 00:05:42.342
of a prospective juror

00:05:42.342 --> 00:05:45.545
that they have the ability to be fair

00:05:45.545 --> 00:05:46.412
and impartial,

00:05:46.412 --> 00:05:47.814
whatever their personal feelings

00:05:47.814 --> 00:05:48.648
might be.

00:05:48.648 --> 00:05:51.517
So do you worry about a sleeper cell?

00:05:51.751 --> 00:05:52.885
Do you worry about somebody

00:05:52.885 --> 00:05:53.553
who is just going to say

00:05:53.553 --> 00:05:55.054
whatever the hell they have to say

00:05:55.054 --> 00:05:56.422
so that they can get on that jury?

00:05:57.790 --> 00:05:59.092
Well, Michael, there's always

00:05:59.092 --> 00:05:59.892
that possibility.

00:05:59.892 --> 00:06:01.227
You're a lawyer you pick juries.

00:06:01.227 --> 00:06:03.496
You know that the process comes up.

00:06:04.130 --> 00:06:05.698
The phrase comes from the French

00:06:05.698 --> 00:06:06.833
voir dire.

00:06:06.833 --> 00:06:08.468
It means to speak the truth

00:06:08.468 --> 00:06:12.238
and the court and the prosecution team

00:06:12.238 --> 00:06:13.373
and the defense team

00:06:13.373 --> 00:06:15.475
gets to individually question

00:06:15.475 --> 00:06:17.477
each prospective juror.

00:06:17.477 --> 00:06:19.245
And we do the best we can.

00:06:19.245 --> 00:06:19.579
You know,

00:06:19.579 --> 00:06:22.615
it may not be the best system ever,

00:06:22.615 --> 00:06:23.916
but it's the best we've got.

00:06:23.916 --> 00:06:28.755
So I believe that after a week

00:06:28.955 --> 00:06:29.889
of questioning,

00:06:29.889 --> 00:06:32.392
hundreds of prospective jurors,

00:06:32.392 --> 00:06:34.093
you know, it sets in some sense, Michael,

00:06:34.093 --> 00:06:37.130
from the set of jurors, the veneer panel

00:06:38.131 --> 00:06:40.299
that we started with I believe we had

00:06:40.299 --> 00:06:42.435
the best subset we could have

00:06:43.536 --> 00:06:44.370
gotten.

00:06:44.370 --> 00:06:46.506
Now, if somebody has a hidden agenda,

00:06:47.073 --> 00:06:47.673
it's hidden.

00:06:47.673 --> 00:06:48.741
We can't figure that out.

00:06:48.741 --> 00:06:49.475
We won't know that.

00:06:49.475 --> 00:06:51.978
But I agree with Michael Van Der Veen.

00:06:52.545 --> 00:06:55.815
I believe that when 12 American citizens

00:06:56.048 --> 00:06:57.550
get into that jury room,

00:06:57.550 --> 00:07:01.187
they really do try to do the right thing.

00:07:01.320 --> 00:07:03.055
I'm a great believer

00:07:03.055 --> 00:07:04.891
in the American jury system,

00:07:04.891 --> 00:07:06.058
and I believe that we

00:07:06.058 --> 00:07:06.926
will get a fair shake.

00:07:08.094 --> 00:07:09.295
And I was thinking as I'm

00:07:09.295 --> 00:07:10.430
speaking to the two of you,

00:07:10.430 --> 00:07:12.632
you know, maybe a change of venue, but

00:07:12.932 --> 00:07:14.333
where are you going to go in the country?

00:07:14.333 --> 00:07:16.302
I mean, these same issues

00:07:16.302 --> 00:07:17.637
would present themselves

00:07:17.637 --> 00:07:18.905
pertaining to Donald Trump

00:07:18.905 --> 00:07:19.739
no matter,

00:07:19.739 --> 00:07:21.207
you know, if you're in Maine

00:07:21.207 --> 00:07:22.942
or if you're in New Mexico.

